The `OpenFailure::Transport` half of #196: the bus's problem, and the command
answers anyway.

#196's premise was half wrong — offline `--registry` already worked, because a
peer session opens against an unreachable endpoint and the union simply fell
back to the files. What it could not survive was a transport that would not
come up at all, which failed a question the files could answer on their own.
Only that second case degrades, and it says so on stderr rather than silently.

Since #501 the explorer session is a **client**, and the two cases are one: a
client whose router does not answer does not open. Zenoh's client defaults are
`connect.timeout_ms: 0` and `connect.exit_on_failure: true`, so the failure is
immediate and without retry; peer mode is `-1` / `false` — retry forever — which
is how a case like this used to become a timeout, or an empty bus, instead of a
test. The fixture still *states* `mode: "client"`, which since #502 is the one
way a file's mode counts — so it pins that path as well.

`blob list` for one producer, a v1 verb whose whole answer is the registry: what
is pinned is the degradation note and the fact that a full answer follows it.
It used to be `topic info`, which FJ4 retired with the rest of v1's registry
nouns (#612); the `--registry` union this pins goes with v1 at FJ9.

```console
$ zenctl blob list --producer netring --format table --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --registry ../fixture-tests/registry
no session (failed to open the Zenoh session); answering from --registry only: ../fixture-tests/registry.
That is what this checkout declares, not what the fleet serves — `zenctl doctor --registry <dir>` compares them when the bus is reachable (RFC 05 §3.1).
declared @blob tiers:

  netring  artifact  v1.1
      endpoints  manifest, slice, have, push, fanout
      reference  BlobReference  (carries the content root — RFC 07 §2.1)
      encoding   application/vnd.tcpdump.pcap
      origins    — (roster not asked)
      packet captures and debug bundles minted by @rpc/netring/artifact
  netring  tree      v1.1
      reference  BlobReference  (carries the content root — RFC 07 §2.1)
      origins    — (roster not asked)
      capture-bundle directory snapshots, keyed by their own root hash
2 of 11 slice(s) declare a tier. A declaration is a capability, never possession — `zenctl blob locate <id>` asks who actually holds one

```

The same answer as ndjson, which is where the note's *placement* shows: it goes
to stderr, so the document on stdout stays parseable — one line per object,
the envelope first.

```console
$ zenctl blob list --producer netring --format ndjson --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --registry ../fixture-tests/registry
no session (failed to open the Zenoh session); answering from --registry only: ../fixture-tests/registry.
That is what this checkout declares, not what the fleet serves — `zenctl doctor --registry <dir>` compares them when the bus is reachable (RFC 05 §3.1).
{"notes":[{"text":"2 of 11 slice(s) declare a tier. A declaration is a capability, never possession — `zenctl blob locate <id>` asks who actually holds one"}],"report":"blob-list","slices_considered":11,"slices_without_blob":9}
{"description":"packet captures and debug bundles minted by @rpc/netring/artifact","encoding":"application/vnd.tcpdump.pcap","endpoints":["manifest","slice","have","push","fanout"],"known_tier":true,"producer":"netring","reference":"BlobReference","registry_version":"1.1","row":"tier","since":"1.8","tier":"artifact"}
{"description":"capture-bundle directory snapshots, keyed by their own root hash","known_tier":true,"producer":"netring","reference":"BlobReference","registry_version":"1.1","row":"tier","since":"1.8","tier":"tree"}
2 of 11 slice(s) declare a tier. A declaration is a capability, never possession — `zenctl blob locate <id>` asks who actually holds one

```

## A verb with nothing to fall back on (#503)

Without `--registry` there is nothing on disk to answer from, and a session
that never opened is not an empty bus. This used to print "no live producers"
and exit 0 — the peer session opened onto nothing. It is the reserved 2 now,
for every verb: nothing was asked, so there is no verdict and no finding. zk2's
`service list` opens its session in the namespace, through the same failure.

```console
$ zenctl service list -c tcp/127.0.0.1:1 --namespace prod
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```

An act included. `pub` printed `published` and a 0 against the same dead
router; nothing went out, and nothing was attempted either — an act's 1 says
it was tried and failed, which is not what happened.

```console
$ zenctl pub v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/sysinfo/health '{}' --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```
